# Post-install guidance for the AUR package.
#
# This lives in a .install file rather than in PKGBUILD. The previous revision defined
# post_install()/post_upgrade()/post_remove() inside PKGBUILD itself, where pacman never
# sees them — they were dead code, and the two steps below (which the package genuinely
# does not work without) were never shown to anyone.
#
# Both steps are unavoidable: reading the hold-to-talk key means reading /dev/input, and
# a packaged daemon cannot add its own user to a group or enable its own unit.

post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'

YazSes is installed. Two manual steps remain — it will not hear you without them:

  1. Grant keyboard access (hold-to-talk reads /dev/input):
        sudo usermod -aG input "$USER"
     Then log out and back in for the group change to apply.

  2. Start the daemon:
        systemctl --user enable --now yazses.service

On Wayland, text injection also needs ydotoold running:
        systemctl --user enable --now ydotoold.service

Then hold the hotkey, speak, release. Config: ~/.config/yazses/config.toml
Verify everything with:  yazses doctor

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    post_install
}

post_remove() {
    cat <<'EOF'

YazSes removed. Per-user state is left in place on purpose; to clear it:

    systemctl --user disable --now yazses.service 2>/dev/null || true
    rm -rf ~/.config/yazses ~/.cache/yazses ~/.local/share/yazses

The last path holds the learning corpus, if you ever enabled it.

EOF
}
